# A scalar in a position the meta-schema says admits a reference. One input per
# line. A blank line and a `#` line are skipped, and `~` on its own is the empty
# string.

# --- a reference ------------------------------------------------------------
$vocabularies.lifecycle_state

# --- a literal --------------------------------------------------------------
~
draft
# A `$` that does not open the scalar is an ordinary character. This is an
# identifier scheme pattern, and it is typed as a string, so nothing would call
# this function on it either.
^DR-[A-Z]{2,6}-[0-9]{4}$

# --- the escape -------------------------------------------------------------
# Quoting cannot escape, because a quoted scalar and a plain one are one value
# under Q2. So the escape is in the sublanguage: `$$` stands for one literal
# `$`, and one `$` is removed from whatever follows.
$$vocabularies.lifecycle_state
$$
$$$x

# --- a malformed reference is an error, not a literal ------------------------
# The alternative reading is worse: a typed root that fell back to a literal
# would put the text `$vocabularise.audience` into a facet's value set, and
# nothing downstream would report it.
$vocabularise.audience
